#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278203 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or above the line $y=3x-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7df3b0c7e884325519ea15f5f949d4b254ef4f63f2449fd36a7aa811ff1b385f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7f/7f710e625802403ca5a34f4331cb2998272479c72deca0e24a6116072e4afb5f.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the equation $y=3x-1.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y\\ge 3x-1.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278208 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=1-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ce1ca6af3caf2b454d7fed484f98d11478839bcf107c0f19562688c7cdaca6a.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-1,3)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/aa/aad8bc7bfd64af0dd1e08618626ae3237bb4aba42be1f91fe7ff9328ad6ecbe7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ satisfy $y=1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278218 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the right of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7dc932c43ed153f4ac1832ca136f9035d879ea1f54e01c69e7a032b7a53d4940.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/06/06fdba4e26ebad28ef3f63106b7bdd8cb115b91a5f0076370a27b12f05added3.png)\n\n \n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ satisfy $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $x>1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278221 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the right of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e5547cb8e0ed0fec1fd2e515a1bcd9009044289dfdc516c6a6cba14b650a7645.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-2,2)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7b/7b353dd43fd293424ba8404184e53869d41d8a1b73493d845bc85aa333130606.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the equation ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${y}^{2}\\ge -2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278222 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/18/181a21e9975320e54365ef10bd801a2fa6494058dd40cda79cb29b713bd8df84.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(3,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/15/15d42de861a3e687a269326e8338ea5d99b6a5a609f4a20e597e64ccc4a8474a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ do not satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278261 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n\n- Statement II is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ (not $4$).\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=2$ (not $3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278301 :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(-1)=(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+1=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}+3(1)^{2}+3(1)+1=8\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+1)({y}^{2}+2y+1) \\\\  & =(y+1)(y+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(y+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has exactly one equilibrium solution,\n\n$$\ny(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278305 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 0,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278339 :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}\\in K$ because \n\n$$\np(0)=(0-1)^{3}=(-1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $K$ such that their sum is *not* in $K.$ For example, let $f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $K$ since $f(0)=g(0)=-1.$ However,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=-1+(0-1)^{3}=-2\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin K.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278340 :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)=(0)^{2}+1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple not in $W.$ For example, let $p(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $p(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $p(0)=1.$ However, \n\n$$\n2\\cdot p(0)=2\\cdot ({0}^{2}+1)=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n Hence, $k\\cdot p(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278341 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in R$ since \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwith $a=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There are matrices in $R$ whose sum is *not* in $R.$ For example, let $A=B={I}_{2}.$ Then $A,B\\in R,$  however,\n\n$$\nA+B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\notin R,\n$$\n\nsince the $11$-entry of this resulting matrix is equal to  $2\\ne 1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278342 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials whose degree is less than or equal to $2,$ however\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡({x}^{3})=3>2.\n$$\n\nSo, $g(x)={x}^{3}\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ then $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 2$ and $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(q(x))\\le 2.$ Since $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)$ is less than or equal to the maximum of the degrees of $p$ and $q$, we deduce that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278349 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{2x}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/0b/0bcadeca7ede6f709bf06391e10da66afd1b514fd0e041bbf108e6e4229b0da6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278396 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto $xy$-plane.  \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2y+1,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278399 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,y)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y).$ Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by $z=2y+1$ and *above* by $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{{2y+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,y)}\\le z\\le {{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto the $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.    \n\n![](images/39/39d2daceb171d26d3396e5aaeab4d8b8da2e9b8324e4042f2b7ed048f4a5fdeb.png)\n\nThe region is bounded on the *left* by $x=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and bounded on the *right* by $x=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,      \n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278403 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6$ onto $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2y+6 & =8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-2y+1) & =4 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=4$ centered at $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278415 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2z+1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+1 & =2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2z-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}-2z+1) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the following ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}\\le 2}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2z+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278433 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 3}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278434 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and *above* by $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{2{x}^{2}+2z-1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=3$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/86/868fceefed2a426feebe2a9199279826aaee935963fec8fa3761b17b97542c34.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=-1$ and bounded *above* by $x=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278505 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98b9dc84e3d23d43c1fef204109d6f6b6c571d6f188592f1f08bb1abf4dc3641.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, and statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the paraboloid is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the cone, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $x$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278508 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and *above* by $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{4}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/46/46757c2bf93af4916afbd019f0faed2eef25245e987acbeff7eafb3e0ca7cd8c.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{3}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278571 :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ff1f735266f66879834e02ca7601e90e40dfa7929e5796d4a3f8cb4ff2828de7.png)\n\n  \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278572 :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e7d8c11ac4b3de8473741a362ad2b78555c764da2d61f955435e8d59771cf46.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278573 :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/84/8414de2e05da43c03fae3c8b1ff2102401e990188e9fd4ee59a24308ec34b086.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. As we have seen, $∂A$ consists of two concentric circles: the outer one is in $A,$ while the inner one is not.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains some of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278575 :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/45/4540e03fc5404474a0cd900c89004e6a5cfe7ac2d4702348e9d0395fc1c914e9.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.  \n\n![](images/2a/2a93b912f0d7b3a84bf0ec9c3b3596d080a9e71a345ff436e2f591edf042d9db.png)\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A$ or, equivalently,  $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278580 :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a380742f47d97062d56b482592cd2d2961dcc40262131a99886b815c507ab6e3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26d264d702368ed6fe05577d7c34630bd16b41bdba8d206dd4db39763db02938.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊈A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278583 :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a39ecae41a88e873fa9eecb30a5b554be9ec98cf630851715ab35f2f873710f2.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and III are false. Since ${C}_{1}⊂∂A-A,$ we must have that $∂A⊄A$ and also that $A\\ne ∂A\\cup \\text{int}A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281284 :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/22/2273cd843c4bfaeaff10d49d3400d7661262673639a66034638d805eef5b1e47.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have  $∂A\\cap A=∅.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III  only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281356 :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is also true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281447 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If $z$ and $w$ are $4$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{4}={w}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{w}^{4}+1=1+1=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4} & =\\frac{{z}^{4}}{{w}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement II, we have that ${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1,$ which means that $\\frac{z}{w}$ is a $4$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the quotient of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281448 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({z}^{3})^{2}={z}^{6}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({z}^{3})^{6} & =({z}^{6})^{3} \\\\  & =(1)^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{3}$ is a $6$th root of unity.\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement I, we have that \n\n$$\n({z}^{3})^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{3}$ is a square root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281449 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $z$ and $w$ are $8$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{8}={w}^{8}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(zw)^{8} & ={z}^{8}{w}^{8} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement II, we have that $(zw)^{8}=1,$ which means that $zw$ is an $8$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the product of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281450 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({z}^{4})}^{2}={z}^{8}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({z}^{4})^{8} & =({z}^{8})^{4} \\\\  & =(1)^{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{4}$ is a $8$th root of unity.\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement I, we have that \n\n$$\n({z}^{4})^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{4}$ is a square root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281451 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If $z$ and $w$ are $4$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{4}={w}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(zw)^{4} & ={z}^{4}{w}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have seen above that $(zw)^{4}=1,$ which means that $zw$ is a $4$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the product of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281452 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $z$ and $w$ are cube roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{3}={w}^{3}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+z)^{3} & =(2z)^{3} \\\\  & ={2}^{3}\\cdot {z}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. From statement II, we have that $(w+w)^{3}=8\\ne 1,$ which means that $w+w=2w$ is *not* a cube root of unity. \n\nIn general, the sum of $n$th roots of unity is *not* an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281455 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If $z$ is a  $4$th root of unity, then (by definition) we have that\n\n$$\n{z}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+z)^{4} & =(2z)^{4} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot {z}^{4} \\\\  & =16\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement II, we have that $(z+z)^{4}\\ne 1,$ which means that $z+z=2z$ is *not* a $4$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281456 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,  since $z$ and $w$ are $6$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{6}={w}^{6}=1.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\nz{w}^{6}=z\\cdot 1=z\\ne w.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(zw)^{6} & ={z}^{6}{w}^{6} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have seen above that $(zw)^{6}=1,$ which means that $zw$ is a $6$th root of unity.  \n\nIn general, the product of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281458 :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have, $p(x)={x}^{3}-1\\notin N$ because\n\n$$\np(0)=(0)^{3}-1=-1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x),g(x)\\in N,$ where $f(0)=0$ and $g(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=0+0=0.\n$$\n\nHence $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281463 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with real entries,   there is a corresponding negative matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice if $A\\in S$ then \n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot |A|=|A|\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any matrices $A,B\\in S,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nA+(B-C)=A+(B+(-C))=(A+B)+(-C)=(A+B)-C\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281464 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as \n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{3}+0\\cdot {x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \nHence, $0\\in U.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)={x}^{3}\\in U$ and the zero-polynomial $q(x)=0\\in U.$ Then \n\n$$\np(x)-q(x)=p(x)-0={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\nq(x)-p(x)=0-p(x)=-{x}^{3}\\ne {x}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have \n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)],\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281487 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and *above* by $x=\\sqrt{2y},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{2y}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the cone is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/d6/d6db5f2d27787c5414f92c2525398b3810d0b0d192ea6837f7cdf483531f48e9.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281509 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,$O=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]$ with $a=b=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, as for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\nA-O=A+(-O)=A+O=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, let's consider the matrices $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then $O$ and ${I}_{2}$ belong to $G,$ and we\nhave \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}-O={I}_{2}\n$$\n\n \nbut \n\n$$\nO-{I}_{2}=-{I}_{2}\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281525 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider the null function $f(x)=0=0{x}^{2}\\in Q.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot 0=0=f(x).\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281526 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $deg(q)$ the degree of a polynomial $q.$ Then, for any $p(x)\\in S$ we have that $(-1)\\cdot p(x)$ is also a polynomial, and that\n\n$$\ndeg((-1)\\cdot p)=deg(-1)+deg(p)=0+deg(p)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)p(x)\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the values $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281527 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in Y,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot {a}_{ij}=-{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=-A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in Y,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Y,$ however $2{I}_{2}\\notin Y$ because\n\n$$\n|2{I}_{2}|=|2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0=4\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284558 :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}–48x+72y+144 & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-48x)+(9{y}^{2}+72y)+144 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-48x)+(9{y}^{2}+72y)+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-12x]+9[{y}^{2}+8y]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}]+9[(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-6)^{2}-36]+9[(y+4)^{2}-16]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}-144+9(y+4)^{2}-144+144 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-6)^{2}-144+9(y+4)^{2}-144+144 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}+9(y+4)^{2} & =-144+144+144 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}+9(y+4)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6>4,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 6=12,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(6,-4).$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, statements II and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284566 :question/worked-solution "We rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3>\\sqrt{3},$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 3=6,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the ellipse is centered at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, statement II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284608 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1-x\\, & ⟹\\,x=1-y \\\\ y=x-1\\, & ⟹\\,x=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/27/27503f983e3e02fa748bb8ef32966a6cd3b5fff29082ffaf3c10d0521b630e93.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le 1-x\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1-|y|\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284661 :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ k\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={k}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284752 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A\\cdot {A}^{T}\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284753 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284754 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284755 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A\\cdot {A}^{T}\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287788 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A\\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c660ff6d042bdf228387e5ad55651f02b136f738a2e4fa99c3ef3b3faef3e3c5.png)\n\nHence, the set $C=A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC={(k,-1),(k,3),(k,5),(m,-1),(m,3),(m,5)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287791 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/1e/1eb13d93fbefc4c7285edef537a9fb3e21339f7a3b37a179eeb150db8d726c1b.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(5,r),(5,s),(5,t),(10,r),(10,s),(10,t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287793 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $X\\times X$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $X.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${X}^{2}=X\\times X$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/17/1718bd6f3f2eaa8dd8fe581b0d938b3b5f1928d5e56a11a324f145e7a481fabe.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times X$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(f,f),(f,g),(f,h),(g,f),(g,g),(g,h),(h,f),(h,g),(h,h)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287795 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $X\\times X$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $X.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${X}^{2}=X\\times X$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7c24108e0d9f5e2e7edbfbe90cdf7318bc247aa73a4c9c72dc61449c7b4da94.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times X$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(k,k),(k,m),(k,n),(m,k),(m,m),(m,n),(n,k),(n,m),(n,n)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287796 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $Z\\times Z$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $Z$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Z.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${Z}^{2}=Z\\times Z$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c58bf1f025cc4960ce3b9dc8e1bc3bf474a521473833b27d136fd77fd6b814a.png)\n\nHence, the set $Y=Z\\times Z$ is given by\n\n$$\nY={(-3,-3),(-3,6),(-3,-9),(6,-3),(6,6),(6,-9),(-9,-3)(-9,6),(-9,-9)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288010 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288024 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-24x+5,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-12x-24,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-12x-24 & =0 \\\\ 12({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x-2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)-(x+2) & =0 \\\\ (x+2)({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,-1,\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-2,\\,-1,\\,1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3(-2)^{4}+8(-2)^{3}-6(-2)^{2}-24(-2)+5=13 \\\\ f(-1) & =3(-1)^{4}+8(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}-24(-1)+5=18 \\\\ f(1) & =3(1)^{4}+8(1)^{3}-6(1)^{2}-24(1)+5=-14 \\\\ f(2) & =3(2)^{4}+8(2)^{3}-6(2)^{2}-24(2)+5=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(2)=45,$ so the global maximum is $45.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-14,$ so the global minimum is $-14.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\n![](images/9b/9b48417439d0e018250328b92561732dd9f4c8bbec157daf33287b20dac7c41f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290257 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=13-9{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-18x+6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =6x(x-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n6x(x-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,3.\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,2]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =13-9(-1)^{2}+2(-1)^{3}=2 \\\\ f(0) & =13-9(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}=13 \\\\ f(2) & =13-9(2)^{2}+2(2)^{3}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=13,$ so the global maximum is $13.$\n\n- The least value is $f(2)=-7,$ so the global minimum is $-7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](images/1a/1a9d2f0db241c0a209c315a8e901e40a44258f339bbaa7bee36198b58fe2684d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290258 :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4{x}^{3}+4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{3}+4x & =0 \\\\ -4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -4x(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-12{x}^{2}+4,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-8<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =4>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=-1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement IV is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290301 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+6x-12 \\\\  & =6({x}^{2}+x-2) \\\\  & =6(x+2)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(x+2)(x-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =6(-3+2)(-3-1)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6(0+2)(0-1)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2+2)(2-1)=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290302 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=4{x}^{2}(4x+3).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(4x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =4(-1)^{2}(4(-1)+3)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4(-\\frac{1}{2})+3)=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4(1)^{2}(4+3)=28>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{4},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{3}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290308 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-2$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+8x-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4x+8.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290311 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+9,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6x-6.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290312 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=4$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+24x-36,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+24.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4) & =-3(4) \\\\  & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290313 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+8,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+2x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12{x}^{2}-12x+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =12(1)^{2}-12(1)+2 \\\\  & =2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290314 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\frac{1}{5}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=-2{x}^{5/3}+{x}^{2/3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2/3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-1/3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{20}{9}{x}^{-1/3}-\\frac{2}{9}{x}^{-4/3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{5}) & =-\\frac{20}{9}\\cdot {5}^{1/3}-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {5}^{4/3}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {5}^{1/3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290351 :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290352 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=4{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}-20x+30,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=20({x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-x-1).\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20(4{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =20(4(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =20(-4+3-1) \\\\  & =-40<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290362 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=2$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-12x+12.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=6x-12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =6\\cdot 2-12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=2.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=1,3$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}-12(1)+12=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-12(3)+12=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=2$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290367 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3/2}-12{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2}+3{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+3{x}^{-3/2}-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{-5/2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {1}^{-1/2}+3\\cdot {1}^{-3/2}-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot {1}^{-5/2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{1/2}-6{x}^{-1/2}+3{x}^{-3/2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{-3/2}(x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point on $(0,\\infty )$ is $x=1.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=\\frac{1}{2},2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3/2}{((\\frac{1}{2})-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3/2}{((-\\frac{1}{2}))}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{{2}^{-3/2}})(\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{3/2}\\cdot {2}^{-2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{-1/2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3\\cdot {2}^{-3/2}(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{-3/2}>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=1$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290368 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-4x+10,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x-4=4(x-1)^{3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$   we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =12(1-1)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find the stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=1.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=0,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =4(0-1)^{3}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4(2-1)^{3}=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative changes the sign at  $x=1$ from minus to plus, we conclude that $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290369 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $X$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $Y$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $Z$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $X$ and $2$ rows in $Y.$ Since the number of columns in $X$ equals the number of rows in $Y,$ the matrices $X$ and $Y$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{X}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{Y}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $Y$ and $2$ rows in $X.$ Since the number of columns in $Y$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $X,$ the matrices $Y$ and $X$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{Y}_{2\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{X}_{2\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $Y$ and $3$ rows in $Z.$ Since the number of columns in $Y$ equals the number of rows in $Z,$ the matrices $Y$ and $Z$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{Y}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{Z}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290370 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $R$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, $S$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, and $T$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere are $4$ columns in $S$ and $4$ rows in $R.$ Since the number of columns in $S$ equals the number of rows in $R,$ the matrices $S$ and $R$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{S}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{✓}{R}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $R$ and $2$ rows in $S.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ equals the number of rows in $S,$ the matrices $R$ and $S$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{S}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $4$ columns in $S$ and $3$ rows in $T.$ Since the number of columns in $S$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $T,$ the matrices $S$ and $T$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{S}_{2\\times 4}{\\times }_{\\ne }{T}_{3\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290371 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $P$ is a $1\\times 5$ row vector, $R$ is a $1\\times 2$ row vector, and $S$ is a $5\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $R$ and $5$ rows in $S.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $S,$ the matrices $R$ and $S$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{S}_{5\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $R$ and $1$ row in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $R$ and $P$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{P}_{1\\times 5}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $5$ columns in $P$ and $5$ rows in $S.$ Since the number of columns in $P$ equals the number of rows in $S,$ the matrices $P$ and $S$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{P}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{5}}}{\\times }_{✓}{S}_{\\boxed{\\text{5}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement III is true, while statements I and II are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290372 :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=0$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=6x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(0)=0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is zero, so we should apply the first derivative test.\n\nLet's evaluate the first derivative at auxiliary points $\\pm 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=0,$ $f(x)$ has no relative extrema.\n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)=0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at $x=0\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290374 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4/3}-24{x}^{1/3}-6{x}^{-2/3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{1/3}-8{x}^{-2/3}+4{x}^{-5/3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{-2/3}+\\frac{16}{3}{x}^{-5/3}-\\frac{20}{3}{x}^{-8/3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (1)^{-2/3}+\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot (1)^{-5/3}-\\frac{20}{3}\\cdot (1)^{-8/3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find the stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{1/3}-8{x}^{-2/3}+4{x}^{-5/3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{-5/3}(4{x}^{2}-8x+4) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{-5/3}{(x-1)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point on $(0,\\infty )$ is $x=1.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=\\frac{1}{2},2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-5/3}{(\\frac{1}{2}-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-5/3}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{{2}^{-5/3}})(\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{5/3}\\cdot {2}^{-2} \\\\  & ={2}^{5/3}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\cdot {2}^{-5/3}(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2-5/3} \\\\  & ={2}^{1/3}>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=1$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290375 :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=2$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=2{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}+10,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=10{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{3}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,2.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=40{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2)=40(2)^{3}-60(2)^{2}=80>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is positive at $x=2,$ so this critical point is a relative *minimum*.\n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=2$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(2)>0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *minimum* at $x=2\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290377 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=2$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-32x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+48x-32 \\\\  & =4({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-8) \\\\  & =4(x-2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12(x-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =12(2-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find the stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-2)^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=2.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=1,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =4(1-2)^{3}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =4(3-2)^{3}=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative changes the sign at $2$  from minus to plus, we conclude that $x=2$  is a relative minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290379 :question/worked-solution "Calculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+4x+16$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+12x+4=4(x+1)^{3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12(x+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ in the second derivative,   we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =12(1-1)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find the stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+1)^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=-1.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=-2,0$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =4(-2+1)^{3}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =4(0+1)^{3}=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative changes the sign at  $x=-1$ from minus to plus, we conclude that $x=-1$  is a local minimum\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290380 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{10},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =4(0)^{3}-6(0)^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1)^{3}(-1-2)=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1)^{3}(1-2)=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =(3)^{3}(3-2)=27>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290381 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+3x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-12x+3=3(2x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=24x-12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =24(\\frac{1}{2})-12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =3(2\\cdot 0-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(2\\cdot 1-1)^{2}=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290382 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=42{x}^{5}-70{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =42(0)^{5}-70(0)^{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ 7{x}^{5}(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =7(-1)^{5}(-1-2)=21>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =7(1)^{5}(1-2)=-7<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =7(3)^{5}(3-2)=7\\cdot {3}^{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290426 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2-2{x}^{2}}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{2-2(0)^{2}}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290427 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{{e}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2\\sqrt{e}≈3.3 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{1}=e≈2.7 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}≈3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(2)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=e,$ so the global minimum is $e.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](images/98/98156f05b9b6c71c7e5a5753d024d0851a0a779741545ba2d62d3d63af41e824.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290428 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-{e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /4}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290433 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =0+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}≈3.8 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )=\\pi ≈3.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](images/53/539dc4ccd2f1b20f521ae0f70bb5d95b8c5f9d1a006ff102baf43ac13ce19660.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290436 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2x+2)-(x-1)\\cdot (2x-2)}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+2-2{x}^{2}+4x-2}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ and $x=2$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{-(-1)^{2}+2(-1)}{((-1)^{2}-2(-1)+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{-(1)^{2}+2(1)}{((1)^{2}-2(1)+2)^{2}}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{-(3)^{2}+2(3)}{((3)^{2}-2(3)+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290551 :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=3x-{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3-3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(1-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3(1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-6x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =6>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=-1,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290566 :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement VI is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290567 :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=15{x}^{2}+30x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{x}^{2}+30x & =0 \\\\ 15x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,-2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=30x+30,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =30>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =-30<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I is true while statement II is false. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is true while statement VI is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=-2,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292684 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+12x+9 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+4x+3) \\\\  & =3(x+1)(x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n3(x+1)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,-1.\n$$\n\nSince $x=-1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,-2]$, the critical points are $x=-3$ and the endpoints $x=-4,-2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}+6(-4)^{2}+9(-4)+3=-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+6(-3)^{2}+9(-3)+3=3 \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2)^{3}+6(-2)^{2}+9(-2)+3=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-3)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-4)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](images/fa/fa85119166ca325f9925ddd03a578fb5daae463c59c3b7b9f7ac0f6464a30241.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292686 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-6x \\\\  & =2x(2{x}^{2}-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2x(2{x}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}$ do not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-3(-1)^{2}+4=2 \\\\ f(0) & =(0)^{4}-3(0)^{2}+4=4 \\\\ f(1) & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}+4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=4,$ so the global maximum is $4.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-1)=f(1)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](images/a7/a7141a342d971d5b520b2f7f07f8fdb1c01ddca18a983fb0c83102a5bd4aee8e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297222 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(0.08)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4e3b5119c92d02bbceb786fefd238182c7c84ca0f4abc85a19f2df86a6ba267.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(0.08)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299715 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305090 :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Smoothness has no bearing on whether the Laplace transform vanishes.\nA function of exponential order always has a well-defined Laplace transform for $s>a$, and there is no reason for that transform to be identically zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The smoothness theorem states that for $f(t)$ of exponential order, the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f}(s)\n$$\n\nis infinitely differentiable at every point in its region of convergence, and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}(s).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The Laplace transform $F(s)$ of a function $f(t)$ is always infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence, even if $f(t)$ is non-smooth.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308441 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{7}{3}y,\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =3{(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{7}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.9$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308442 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=5,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}y,\\,y(0)=5.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)((1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =5{(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{3}{2}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{3}{2}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=1$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308443 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{9}{4}y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{9}{4}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{9}{4}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)((1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{9}{4}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{9}{4}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.6$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308452 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=6,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{3}y,\\,y(0)=6.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{8}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{8}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =6{(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{8}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{8}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.8$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308461 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-6y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-6{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+6{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+6Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}.\n$$\n\nThis shows that the statement I is false.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+6Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.10.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308464 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-5{y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-5Δx|}{|2+5Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-5Δx| & <|2+5Δx| \\\\ (2-5Δx)^{2} & <(2+5Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} & <4+20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -20Δx & <20Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=1,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308466 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-2{y}_{n}+(-2{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(2Δx)=\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|1-Δx|}{|1+Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |1-Δx| & <|1+Δx| \\\\ (1-Δx)^{2} & <(1+Δx)^{2} \\\\ 1-2Δx+(Δx)^{2} & <1+2Δx+(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -2Δx & <2Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=2,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308467 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-7{y}_{n}+(-7{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(7Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-7Δx|}{|2+7Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-7Δx| & <|2+7Δx| \\\\ (2-7Δx)^{2} & <(2+7Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} & <4+28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -28Δx & <28Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308534 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}-4({y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-8{y}_{n}+16{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+8{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}<Δx-\\frac{1}{4} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it does not satisfy this inequality, the modified Euler method is unstable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308536 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let’s check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation  \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-2{y}_{n}-2({y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}+4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+2{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}<Δx-\\frac{1}{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0<Δx & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and hence statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308691 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(1+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is finite, and $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{x}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, while $3x\\to 0.$\nSo\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{4}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308693 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{5}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{5}-2x}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(3{x}^{5}-2x) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{5}-2x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(3{x}^{3}-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{5}-2x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308694 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used the fact that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1.$ Hence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, near $x=0$ we know that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x≈x,$ so $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}$ behaves like $\\frac{1}{x},$ which becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308697 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}≈\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3x+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈1$ near $x=0,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3x+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308698 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}≈1.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x})=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x$ near $x=0,$ the quotient \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nbecomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308700 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have ${e}^{x}≈1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}≈\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nThus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}≈1$ near $x=0,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308701 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{8}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{8}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5x+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308703 :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence.\n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every $s>a,$\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous, there exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The standard existence theorem for the Laplace transform says that if $f$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and of exponential order $a,$ then the improper integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges for every $s>a.$ This is exactly what it means for $F(s)$ to exist for all $s>a.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The smoothness theorem guarantees\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}\n$$\n\nfor every $n\\ge 0$ and each $s>a,$ which shows that $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable, even if $f(t)$ itself is not.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308715 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{2}-x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x(3x-1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x(3x-1)}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince now direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3x-1)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308716 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{2}+5+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308717 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\infty +0 \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311951 :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314968 :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+6)=f((x+3)+3)=f(x+3)=f(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true by definition of the periodic extension.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+1)=f(3)=F(3)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315068 :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi /2$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Using the periodicity of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, any shift by a multiple of its period returns the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f((x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides equal $f(x),$ the statement is true for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315069 :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\nf(x+4\\pi )=f((x+2\\pi )+2\\pi )=f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=2\\pi .$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2\\pi ) & =f(0+2\\pi )=f(0)=F(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\nF(2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317237 :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-4){y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x)=(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{5x}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}Q(x)=(x-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{(x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=2$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319314 :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The matrix $A$ has $1$ pivot column and $1$ non-pivot column. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319352 :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The matrix $A$ has $3$ pivot columns and $2$ non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $B$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323674 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323750 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. Again,  according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}]}
